{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___? I'm Dr. Patel, one of the hospitalists. I've been reviewing your case. You came in after passing out, is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s correct. I felt dizzy getting off the plane, then just blacked out. I’ve been feeling faint for days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Your wife mentioned your blood pressure drops when you stand. We call that orthostatic hypotension. We’ll look into why that’s happening."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been holding my blood pressure meds—lisinopril, carvedilol, and the diuretics—because I thought they were making me dizzy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We’ll check your labs and monitor your vitals. We also need to rule out serious causes like a heart issue or pulmonary embolism."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had a pulmonary embolism before. I’m on warfarin. Is that still okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:51:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR was 1.9 on arrival—just below therapeutic. We’ve bridged you with Lovenox and increased your warfarin to 7.5 mg daily to get it back in range."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ve also had this chest tightness and shortness of breath when I walk."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we saw that. Your EKG showed sinus tachycardia and some extra beats, but no signs of a heart attack. Troponin was negative."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:54:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:55:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We can’t rule it out completely, but your stress test and echo will help. Given your diabetes and weight, heart disease is a concern, but so far, no clear evidence."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:56:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my neck? It’s been swollen and tender for weeks. Feels worse now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:57:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We noticed that. Your parotid and submandibular glands are enlarged. We did a CT of your neck and chest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:58:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is it infected again? I had sialadenitis before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2178-02-11 21:59:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT shows no abscess or inflammation. The glands are fatty-replaced and symmetrically enlarged—unchanged from your last scan. No signs of bacterial infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2178-02-11 22:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what is it? Cancer? Something serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2178-02-11 22:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No signs of cancer. ENT reviewed the images and thinks it’s likely fatty infiltration, possibly related to your long-term steroid use. No biopsy needed now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2178-02-11 22:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Steroids? I’ve been on dexamethasone for years for my arthritis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2178-02-11 22:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. That’s also why you have adrenal insufficiency. Your body doesn’t make its own cortisol anymore. We’ll keep you on your 4.5 mg daily dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2178-02-11 22:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had nosebleeds too. Should I worry?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2178-02-11 22:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They stopped on their own, and your blood count is stable. Likely from dry air or irritation. We’ll keep an eye on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs from last night show your white count is a bit high, and your LDH is very elevated—897."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "LDH? What does that mean?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a marker of cell turnover. Could be from muscle, liver, heart, or even tumors. But your liver enzymes are only mildly up, and no heart damage. No lymph nodes on imaging."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what could it be?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re not sure. Could be related to your chronic inflammation or steroid use. We’ve sent an AFP to check for rare causes like germ cell tumors. It’s pending."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my dizziness? Any answers?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We gave you a fluid bolus, but no improvement. Endocrinology says your steroid dose is already high, so adrenal crisis is unlikely. We’re holding your diuretics for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2178-02-12 08:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll do a nuclear stress test today to check your heart. And we’ll repeat the LDH and INR tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2178-02-13 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The stress test came back normal. No signs of ischemia. Your heart function is likely okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2178-02-13 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. But I still feel faint when I stand."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2178-02-13 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re considering if your enlarged neck tissues are pressing on the carotid sinus, which could cause fainting. ENT will see you as an outpatient."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2178-02-13 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds strange. Is that possible?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2178-02-13 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s rare, but possible. We’ll refer you for a tilt table test and neurology consult after discharge to explore that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2178-02-13 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We got the neck CT results. Still shows the same fatty parotid enlargement. No new lesions or inflammation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2178-02-13 14:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not getting worse?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2178-02-13 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, it’s stable. We’re not treating it as an infection since antibiotics didn’t help before. We’ll follow up with ENT."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your repeat labs show LDH is still high—720—but trending down. INR is now 2.7, which is perfect."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the warfarin is working?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’ll keep you on 7.5 mg daily. We’re restarting furosemide, but holding HCTZ and spironolactone for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Why stop those?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They might be contributing to your dizziness. We’ll see how you do without them and adjust based on your weight and swelling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My leg wound—is it healing?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2178-02-14 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the dressing is clean, no signs of infection. You’re off antibiotics, and wound care will continue at the extended care facility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow. Your condition is stable, and we’ve ruled out major issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Where am I going?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "To an extended care facility for a few days. They’ll help with mobility, monitor your blood pressure, and manage your meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll see ENT for your neck, cardiology for the stress test results, and your rheumatologist. We’re also checking AFP and scheduling a tilt table test."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my steroids? Should I keep taking them?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2178-02-15 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue the dexamethasone. Endocrinology and rheumatology are discussing starting cyclosporine to possibly reduce your steroid dose long-term."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Today’s the day. You’re being discharged to the extended care facility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What changes to my meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stop HCTZ and spironolactone for now. Warfarin is now 7.5 mg daily. Keep all your other medications the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the nosebleeds or dizziness?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Report any severe nosebleeds. For dizziness, check your weight daily and avoid sudden standing. The outpatient workup will help clarify things."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel better just knowing what’s going on."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and follow up closely. We’re here if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2178-02-16 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2178-02-11 21:43:00",
    "admission_end": "2178-02-16 13:55:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old male with a history of psoriatic arthritis, secondary adrenal insufficiency, morbid obesity, and prior pulmonary embolism was admitted for syncope and dizziness. He had been experiencing orthostatic symptoms and an episode of fainting after standing, prompting evaluation for cardiac, metabolic, and neurologic causes. Workup revealed a subtherapeutic INR, which was corrected with warfarin dose adjustment and bridging anticoagulation. Cardiac testing, including a nuclear stress test and echocardiogram, showed no evidence of ischemia or significant dysfunction, though image quality was limited by body habitus. His chronic bilateral parotid and submandibular gland enlargement, consistent with fatty infiltration and sialadenitis, remained stable on imaging with no signs of infection or malignancy. LDH was markedly elevated without clear etiology, prompting further outpatient evaluation. Diuretics were held due to persistent dizziness, with furosemide restarted before discharge while spironolactone and HCTZ were discontinued. He was discharged to extended care with plans for outpatient ENT, neurology, and cardiology follow-up, along with monitoring of INR, electrolytes, and pending AFP results.",
    "problems": [
      "Syncope and collapse",
      "Psoriatic arthropathy",
      "Other adrenal hypofunction",
      "Sialoadenitis",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Personal history of pulmonary embolism",
      "Nonspecific elevation of levels of transaminase or lactic acid dehydrogenase [LDH]",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Epistaxis",
      "Adjustment disorder with anxiety",
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Polyneuropathy in diabetes",
      "Open wound of knee, leg [except thigh], and ankle, without mention of complication"
    ]
  }
}
